course: dictionary
lesson: 2490
title: Deafen and deafening
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Deafen is a verb. It means to make a noise so loud that people cannot hear anything else, or to damage someone's hearing so they cannot hear well. «The music deafened us» means the music was so loud we could hardly hear anything else.
explain: Deafening is the form you will meet in stories, as in «a deafening roar». It describes a noise so loud that it fills all your hearing. Authors also use «a deafening silence», a silence so noticeable that it feels almost loud.
explain: Context helps you work out the meaning. «The explosion deafened him in one ear» tells you the sound damaged his hearing, because an explosion is not something you just listen to. To check, swap in your meaning: if "damaged his hearing" still makes sense in the sentence, you have it.
fact: Deafen means to make a noise so loud that people cannot hear, or to damage someone's hearing.
fact: «Deafening» describes a noise so loud that it is hard to hear anything else.
fact: «A deafening silence» is a silence that is very noticeable, often after something awkward.
fact: Very loud noises can damage the tiny hair cells in the inner ear, which is why ear defenders and earplugs protect hearing at concerts, fireworks and in noisy jobs.
fact: «Roosters crow at many times of day, not only at dawn, and a rooster partly closes its ear canals when crowing so it does not deafen itself.»
exercise: «The music deafened us.» What does deafened mean here? | Played it very softly | Made us dance all night | *Made it so loud we could hardly hear anything else | Turned it off
exercise: «The factory made a deafening roar all day.» What does deafening describe? | *The roar, which was so loud it filled our hearing | The day of the week | The factory's windows | A small, quiet hum
exercise: «The explosion deafened him in one ear.» Why might the author use deafened here? | It shows he was pleased with the noise | *It shows the sound damaged his hearing | It shows the noise was quiet and gentle | It shows he went straight to sleep
test: Which word means a noise so loud that it is hard to hear anything else? | Barely | *Deafening | Curt | Glum
test: What does «a deafening silence» suggest? | A quiet, peaceful sleep | A bright light in the dark | *A silence that is very noticeable, often after something awkward | A silence in a library
exam: Mia says deafen means to make someone laugh. What does deafen really mean? | To make someone laugh very hard | To wear a hat in the cold | To say something very softly | *To make a noise so loud that people cannot hear, or to damage someone's hearing
